WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.912
Your AI agent is brilliant for an hour.

2
00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:08.425
Then the session ends, and it forgets your projects, your decisions, and how you like things done.

3
00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:10.000
Every single time.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.537
You end up explaining it all over again.

5
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.700
AI SecondBrain Stack fixes that with a folder.

6
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.500
A git-versioned second brain your agent reads at the start of every session and writes back to at the end.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.850
Four agents, one rulebook.

8
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:28.250
No dashboard to learn, no subscription, just files you already own.

9
00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.245
Setup is one paste.

10
00:00:30.245 --> 00:00:40.482
You hand the bootstrap prompt to your agent and it becomes the installer: it checks your environment, interviews you about what you actually work on, and builds the structure around your answers.

11
00:00:40.482 --> 00:00:42.307
No wizard to click through.

12
00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:44.307
Your agent does the setup work.

13
00:00:44.426 --> 00:00:49.201
Then it wires up the skills you keep repeating, and only the servers you asked for.

14
00:00:49.201 --> 00:00:52.401
Your API keys stay in your agent's own config.

15
00:00:52.401 --> 00:00:55.488
Never in a note, never in a commit.

16
00:00:55.488 --> 00:01:00.776
It is a rule the installer states up front and repeats at every step that touches a key.

17
00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.790
It can plan your backup, and it keeps itself current every month.

18
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.940
Everything is reversible, because everything is git.

19
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.752
No lock-in, no proprietary format, nothing to migrate later.

20
00:01:12.752 --> 00:01:14.640
Start with one paste.
